# Caldera Mortar — sculpting and rigging brief

You are sculpting and rigging the Caldera Mortar, the squat lobbing tower of the Holdfast
— a heavy, wide brass base carrying one fat, short, wide-mouthed iron tub angled steeply
upward — as a 3D voxel model with a rig a game can pose at runtime. Build it with
`voxel-anim` (painting opaque cells one operation at a time) as a rigged model that reads
unmistakably as this stubby lobbing emplacement, rocks gently on its own, and recoils hard
as it lobs a shell on demand. There is no target model to copy.

This test measures creativity and craft, not instruction-following. The brief fixes what
the Mortar is and how it must move; it deliberately does not hand you a parts list, joint
placements, or pose angles — working out the pieces a tower whose tub elevates and recoils
needs, where they attach, and how they articulate is the test. Invent the rig.

## The volume and coordinate system

- The volume is **{{voxel.width}} wide (x) × {{voxel.height}} tall (y) × {{voxel.depth}} deep (z)**, in opaque voxels. It
  starts empty.
- x runs across the tower, `0`–`{{voxel.maxX}}`. y runs up, `0` (bottom, the ground) to `{{voxel.maxY}}`
  (top). z runs front-to-back, `0`–`{{voxel.maxZ}}`.
- Forward is +z: the tub faces toward `z = {{voxel.maxZ}}` (the front), though its fat mouth is
  angled steeply **up** toward `y = {{voxel.maxY}}` so it lobs on an arc. Up is +y.
- The Mortar is a **static emplacement founded on the ground**: its heavy base sits flat on
  the ground at `y = 0` and does not move. Build it symmetric about the lengthwise vertical
  centerplane.
- The Mortar is deliberately the **shortest and squattest** tower — a low, wide, heavy
  base with a stubby tub, filling the volume near the ground and never rising tall. Nothing
  about it should read as slender or towering.
- Each part is sculpted separately with `voxel-anim --part <name>`, in this same volume's
  coordinates, positioned where the part sits on the assembled Mortar (the tub already
  seated on the base, angled up).

## Palette

Use only these opaque colors:

| Role | Hex |
| --- | --- |
| Brass — primary plating | `#c8a24a` |
| Brass dark — shadow, underside | `#8a6d2e` |
| Iron — mechanisms, barrels, recesses | `#3a3836` |
| Accent fitting — verdigris (the accent region) | `#2f7d72` |

Do **not** use steel `#b8bcc2`, gold `#ffce54`, or white `#dfeaea` anywhere on the model.
Those are the colors a game paints onto the accent band at run time to show the tower's
upgrade level (see *The accent region*); baking any of them in makes a base-level tower
look permanently upgraded.

## What the Mortar is (and what is yours to invent)

Fixed — the Mortar must read unmistakably as all of these:

- A **heavy, wide brass base** founded flat on the ground — the squat, solid body of the
  shortest tower on the field. Its shadowed seams and undersides are brass dark.
- **One fat, short, wide-mouthed iron tub** angled steeply upward on the base — a stubby
  lobbing barrel with a broad, flared mouth and **no length to it at all**. This one fat,
  short muzzle pointing up is the Mortar's whole tell: a reviewer must be able to name it
  from that stubby upward tub alone.
- A **muzzle band** ringing the flared mouth of the tub — the accent region below.

The Mortar is a **weapon of the Holdfast — brass-and-iron machinery, not a creature**. The
tell is the **one fat, short, wide-mouthed tub** angled steeply up on a squat base: nothing
about this tower is slim, towering, or barrel-less.

Everything else is yours to invent — the exact silhouette and proportions of the base and
tub, how the mouth flares, how the tub is mounted so it can elevate and recoil, how the
band is formed, and how you break the Mortar into rig parts. Nothing here prescribes a
shape; the test rewards a bold, characterful design that is unmistakably the Mortar and
animates convincingly.

## The accent region — a hard contract

A game recolors this model at run time to show the tower's **upgrade level**, by finding
every voxel of one reserved color and repainting it (brass dark at level 0, steel at level
1, gold at level 2), so one model serves all three levels. That reserved color is the
**verdigris accent** `#2f7d72`, and it is what makes the contract work:

- Sculpt the **muzzle band** ringing the flared mouth of the tub — and only that — in
  `#2f7d72`.
- Use `#2f7d72` **nowhere else** on the model. Not a stray voxel, not a highlight, not a
  seam. Every voxel of it is treated as accent fitting.
- The band must be **one contiguous ring** and clearly **visible from more than one angle**
  — that is how a player reads a tower's upgrade level at a glance across the battlefield.

Everything else — the base, the tub, the recesses — keeps its own color and is never
repainted. Get this wrong and every Mortar in the game looks the same level.

## The required animations — the fixed contract

`rig.json` is pre-seeded with two required animation declarations by name (you author the
motion). Author each with `voxel-anim define-animation` then `add-keyframe`, choosing the
period and each key's `--interp` (`constant`/`linear`/`bezier` or
`ease-in`/`ease-out`/`ease-in-out`, with optional `--in-handle`/`--out-handle`) so the
motion reads right — a slow, gentle rock at idle and a heavy elevate-and-recoil when firing.

- **`idle`** — the settle (a self-playing idle, `loop = true`, `auto_play = true`). The tub
  rocks very slightly on its trunnions, settling and re-settling as if breathing, on its own
  with no caller, so the tower reads as live and powered even when it is not firing. Only the
  tub rocks; the heavy base stays fixed. It loops seamlessly.
- **`fire`** — the lob (a game-triggered playable, `loop = true`). The tub elevates, then
  recoils hard down into its base as it lobs a shell, and slowly returns to its resting
  elevation, reading as a stubby mortar throwing a shell on an arc rather than a flat direct
  shot. The base stays fixed; only the tub moves.

You may add your own extra parts, joints, or animations on top of this (for example a faint
plume vent at rest), but you must not drop or contradict the required `idle` and `fire`
animations, by those names.

## Working the tool

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already on your
`PATH`. Define your own parts with `define-part` (giving each a parent and a pivot), sculpt
each by selecting it with `--part <name>`, set pivots with `set-pivot`, place joints with
`define-joint`, and author the two animations' keyframes with
`define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. Run `voxel-anim --help` for the available operations
(setting and clearing single voxels, filling and stroking boxes, 3D lines, spheres, and a
mirror plane), the rig subcommands, and the animation subcommands, and `voxel-anim
<operation> --help` for each one's exact flags. Call `voxel-anim` once per operation; run
`voxel-anim render` and read `parts/<part>.png` (and the assembled `scene/*.png` previews)
between calls to judge each part against this brief — that the tub seats on the base angled
up, its mouth flares broad, the band rings the mouth unbroken, and the animations read with
the right weight. Run `voxel-anim render` before you finish so it emits the per-part `.glb`
geometry your result is built from — an unrendered part scores as empty (`voxel-anim render
--component <part>` renders one part; `voxel-anim render --time <ms> --animation <name>`
renders the model posed at that instant to check the motion).
